    <ImageXex>
      <!-- Files carried inside the XEX, so Omni360.xex is the only file to
           deploy. Each name=file becomes an imagexex /section: switch.

             font    the console font atlas, read by ATG::Console as
                     embed:\font
             selawk  the UI font, selawk.ttf byte for byte. UiText.cpp
                     loads it through XUI's section:// transport.
             ofl     the SIL Open Font License text. The OFL lets the font
                     be embedded in software provided the copyright notice
                     and licence travel with every copy - this is them
                     travelling with it.

           Section names are capped at 8 characters. -->
      <AdditionalSections>font=$(OutDir)Media\Fonts\font.xpr;selawk=$(ProjectDir)Media\Fonts\selawk.ttf;ofl=$(ProjectDir)Media\Fonts\OFL.txt</AdditionalSections>
      <!-- Omni360's own TitleID, moved off X-Store's so the two can coexist on
           one console. A shared TitleID means a shared save and content
           namespace, which is a real conflict now this is a separate app
           rather than a fork running in X-Store's place.

           THE 0x PREFIX IS LOAD-BEARING. imagexex parses this value as
           DECIMAL unless it starts with 0x. A bare hex string containing
           A-F is rejected:

               error IM1059: invalid value ('FFFF0360') for titleid

           Verified directly against the tool rather than by rebuilding -
           imagexex validates its arguments before it opens the input file,
           so you can test a value in a second without a hardware cycle:

               imagexex.exe /titleid:<value>

           IM1059 means the value is bad. IM1005 ("missing required option
           '/IN:'") means it parsed fine and the tool simply got no input,
           which is the result you want. 95126000, 12345678, 0xFFFF0360 and
           0xffff0360 all pass; FFFF0360, 4F4D0360, FFFF07A7 and FFFF do not.
           There is no range check at all - 99999999999999 is accepted.

           Worth knowing: X-Store's own TitleID is written 95126000, which
           has no 0x, so it is decimal. The title ID it actually ships is
           0x05AB81F0, NOT 0x95126000 as the digits suggest. Anything
           comparing against X-Store's real ID wants 0x05AB81F0.

           FFFF here is convention only (unsigned homebrew usually sits in
           the FFFE/FFFF range); imagexex does not enforce it. -->
      <TitleID>0xFFFF0360</TitleID>
    </ImageXex>
    <Deploy>
      <DeploymentType>CopyToHardDrive</DeploymentType>
